 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Pollination is the transfer of pollen grains from an anther to a stigma.\n• Self-pollination: pollen goes to the stigma of the same flower or another flower on the same plant.\n• Cross-pollination: pollen goes to a flower on another plant of the same kind; it gives more variation.\nAgents of pollination: insects (bees, butterflies), wind, birds, bats, and people (hand pollination of vanilla or oil palm).\nInsect-pollinated flowers: large, colourful petals, scent and nectar, sticky or rough pollen, anthers and stigmas inside the flower (hibiscus, flamboyant).\nWind-pollinated flowers: small, dull, no scent or nectar, lots of light smooth pollen, long filaments and feathery stigmas hanging outside (maize, grasses).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "f": "maize"
  },
  "problem": "{f} flowers are small and dull, with feathery stigmas (the silks) and anthers hanging outside. How are they pollinated? Explain.",
  "steps": [
   "These features suit the wind: there is no need to attract insects.",
   "Anthers hanging out let the wind blow the light pollen away; feathery stigmas catch pollen from the air."
  ],
  "answer": "By wind: hanging anthers release pollen; feathery stigmas catch it.",
  "checks": []
 },
